\paragraph{Results.}
The detector identifies all three CIVO-positive runs (TPR = 3/3). Two of three are detected \emph{predictively}: 008d seed~0 triggers at 45K, one checkpoint before evaluation crosses the 20\% decline threshold; seed~3 triggers at 45K, a full 50K steps before decline begins. The third case (010b, Cheetah Run) involves single-step collapse (evaluation drops 80\% in one interval), and the detector fires reactively at the next available checkpoint. No checkpoint-based monitor can provide advance warning for instantaneous collapse.

Two false positives occur among seven negative cases (FPR = 2/7, precision = 3/5 = 0.60, F$_1$ = 0.75). Run 009c (no duration embedding) develops genuine late-stage value overestimation unrelated to CIVO; the detector correctly identifies value-performance dissociation, and the alert is arguably useful despite the label mismatch. Run 009a (vanilla baseline) triggers during a transient three-checkpoint eval dip at 85K; the model subsequently recovers to 966, constituting a pure false alarm. In practice, the cost of a false alert (manual checkpoint inspection, minutes) is orders of magnitude lower than the cost of undetected collapse (wasted GPU-hours, corrupted model); moderate FPR is therefore acceptable in this application.

All three true positives triggered before 65K steps (range 45--65K) while both false positives occurred after 85K (range 85--105K), suggesting that early-training alerts carry higher diagnostic confidence. In practice, alerts before 70K steps can be treated as high-confidence CIVO indicators; later alerts warrant manual inspection.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
Four constraints bound the current detector. First, the three-checkpoint window cannot distinguish transient dips from sustained collapse (the source of the 009a false alarm). Second, the acceleration condition assumes monotonically increasing overestimation during onset; oscillatory trajectories that eventually collapse could evade detection until acceleration stabilizes. Third, the detector identifies a \emph{pattern} (rising value-evaluation ratio with declining performance) rather than a \emph{mechanism}: run 009c shows that non-CIVO pathologies produce the same signature. Fourth, threshold values ($\tau_\text{mag} = 0.49$, $\tau_\text{vel} = 0.1$) were calibrated on ten runs across two environments; generalization to different reward scales requires further validation.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:methodology}

\subsection{Base Architecture}
\label{sec:base_arch}

All experiments build on DreamerV3 \citep{hafner2023dreamerv3}, which learns a recurrent state-space model (RSSM) with discrete latent states ($32$ categories $\times$ $32$ classes), uses symlog-transformed predictions, and trains an actor-critic entirely within imagined trajectories. The standard configuration imagines $H{=}15$ steps at a fixed temporal resolution of $\dt{=}1$, meaning each imagination step corresponds to one environment transition.

\subsection{Variable-Rate Imagination Mechanism}
\label{sec:variable_rate}

We introduce three orthogonal architectural modifications that enable variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3. Each can be independently toggled to isolate its contribution.

\paragraph{Temporal embedding.}
A learned embedding $e(\dt)$ is concatenated to the RSSM input at each imagination step, conditioning state predictions on the temporal scale. We test three modes:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Trained}: embedding parameters are updated via backpropagation through the world model loss, allowing the RSSM to co-adapt its dynamics and reward predictions to the temporal scale.
\item \textbf{Frozen}: a fixed random projection of identical dimensionality (\texttt{requires\_grad=False}). The RSSM receives a temporal signal but cannot co-adapt to it.
\item \textbf{Removed}: embedding dimension set to zero; no temporal conditioning signal is provided.
\end{itemize}
We further distinguish \textbf{trained-and-exposed} (subsample ON: the RSSM trains on $\dt{>}1$ transitions) from \textbf{trained-but-unexposed} (subsample OFF: $\dt{>}1$ used only during imagination). This four-way classification---trained/exposed, trained/unexposed, frozen, removed---is the key causal structure of our factorial design.

\paragraph{Temporal subsampling.}
When enabled, the replay buffer augments training data with variable-$\dt$ transitions by subsampling consecutive frames. Concretely, with a maximum subsampling factor of~2, the RSSM trains on both $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ transitions drawn from the same episodes. When disabled, only standard $\dt{=}1$ transitions are used. This factor controls whether the world model has seen multi-scale dynamics during training.

\paragraph{Imagination horizon and step size.}
The imagination horizon $H$ sets the number of rollout steps. We test $H{=}15$ (default) and $H{=}8$. The reduced horizon controls for compounding error: $H{=}8$ steps at $\dt{=}2$ covers $16$ physical time steps, comparable to the $15$ physical steps of the default configuration. Additionally, the parameter \texttt{gate\_fixed\_dt} forces all imagination steps to use a specific $\dt$ value ($1$, $2$, or $4$), separating the effect of $\dt$-conditioning from any learned gating policy.

\subsection{Factorial Design}
\label{sec:factorial}

We adopt a structured factorial ablation across four factors: $\dt$-embedding mode (trained / frozen / none), temporal subsampling (on / off), imagination horizon ($H{=}15$ / $H{=}8$), and imagination step size ($\dt{=}1$, $2$, or $4$). Table~\ref{tab:factorial_design} summarizes the experimental configurations.

